00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
let's stick with that theme of schools  ahead of the big return to the classroom  tomorrow and we're joined by the ofsted  chief inspector amanda spellman thank  you very much for being on the programme  um i just want to start with what you've  written in the sun on sunday today  and saying it's great the school gates  will be thrown open next week but we  know the school experience won't be  normal for some time yet  testing face masks do you think that  pupils and  parents are really ready for some of the  logistical challenges  i think the vast majority are they had a  bit of a taster of this last term  um i think what we can see about the  proportion of parents who are expecting  to have their children back in school  the first possible day  shows um that people can cope do you  think that children  are going to be prepared i guess for  wearing face masks not just in communal  areas like  corridors but all day in the classroom  children are adaptable and flexible and  i think the overwhelming  um thing we're seeing is that the vast  majority of parents the vast majority  of children vast majority of teachers  are really happy  to be going back to school i think they  can live with a little bit of  inconvenience for a few weeks  i really hope um that the whole  paraphernalia of masks  and testing isn't only necessary for a  short time  i think we've got some reasons to be  optimistic from what we can see  um but let's make sure that children get  the pleasure of learning the pleasure of  socializing  and the pleasure of all those wider  activities they get when they're at  school  i was talking to gavin williamson a bit  about what happens if children  just don't wear face masks and do you  think that they need to be  enforced in the classroom or do you  think that the current approach is the  right one  face masks testing this is part of a  system of infection control that's  not designed or expected to eliminate  any infection ever happening  it's designed to reduce it um to levels  that should that  shouldn't cause anybody anxiety so  of course they're always going to be a  small proportion of children and of  adults who can't wear face masks  um but we shouldn't we we shouldn't let  anxieties about a tiny proportion  stop us recognizing um the overall goal  here  it's interesting as well to hear you  talk about how you hope that this will  be for a limited amount of time  what kind of period are you hoping that  it will last for before  some of these measures can be eased well  i think we've been told that  the face mask guidance will be reviewed  at easter i think so  i i love the idea of children being able  to come back in the summer term  able able to see everybody fully okay  now often of course began inspecting  schools again in january checks had  previously been  suspended because of covert what kind of  things  are your inspections finding well we  reported through the autumn we did  interim visits that weren't judgment  visits and we're doing monitoring visits  now  what we've seen this term has shown the  extraordinary efforts that schools have  been putting into remote education it's  been  very hard work for teachers and of  course as we know not as  nothing like as rewarding on the  receiving end so it's one of the reasons  why we should all be very happy about  getting back to school  but schools have been thinking about  what it is is the  is the the minimum curriculum they want  to make sure that children get even when  they're working remotely  a lot of effort has gone into making  sure that almost all children  are able to to reach the curriculum  through through one kind of device or  another um but it has been a slog  it's been a real slog people are  children at the receiving end are bored  lonely miserable  anxious and really really want the  normal experience again  yeah you can definitely see slog is  probably the right word there  how worried are you about the mental  health aspect of this as well you know  we talk an awful lot about  academic achievement about exams about  catch-up and progress but  it does strike me that you know it's  been very hard for  young people mentally from a mental  health perspective  and all this time at home cut off from  their friends staring at the computer  it has been very hard and i just said  that  lonely bored miserable that's very very  common but i think the good news is that  for the vast majority of children  the restoration of normality and  everyday experience of  the sense of learning being with friends  being with teachers  um getting getting to play football or  whatever it might happen to be again  that of itself for most children will be  enough  to lift those symptoms to get to to get  them back into into into being the  children um they were before and will be  again but  there is a there is a minority um let's  hope it's not too large a minority  who whose problems have increased and  it's really important that  we we're good at recognizing uh where  problems are arising things like eating  disorders things like self-harm  i know that mental health services are  very aware of the  the kinds of problems that have been  increasing and where they can expect  more more cases coming through so  everybody needs to be  alert to these the education secretary  and  gavin williamson was pretty clear that  changes to the school holidays  are on the table that they could be  looking at a five-term year potentially  or shortening the summer holiday that's  one of the things  that this review is looking into you've  written today saying making school days  longer or school holidays shorter could  backfire why do you think it could  backfire  well first of all i really want to make  sure that all children do get their  their full  full ration of schooling and we don't  want children to lose out in the long  term they're  they've been postponed to the interests  of the old and the sick while we've  dealt with coronavirus but  but children really matter and their  lives matter  um in terms of adjustments to to to  school days and school years  um many experiments have happened in the  past um i think one of the really  important things is to learn from the  experiments that have happened in the  past because  of course there's no put there's no  point adding time here or moving time  there  if you get if you don't get a  groundswell of support if children  simply don't turn up  for extra time or summer schools for  example you could end up putting a lot  of effort  into something that doesn't achieve the  objective so my concern is to make sure  that we go  with the grain of what parent parents  will embrace  um to make sure that all children get  the very most out of their education  you talk about what experiments have  shown previously  and what have they shown with with  regards to holidays  i think a number of schools have  experimented over the last couple of  decades with things like five  term years i don't think many of those  have persisted i don't know the reasons  why  um but but i think it's really important  to to learn if people and if people are  proposing  um to redesign to make sure that people  understand  um why they haven't been a long-standing  success  in the past and make make sure they're  designed in ways that people really will  throw themselves into supporting okay  and  on social media quite a few people have  contacted me with very different  experiences of home schooling and the  kind of  lessons that their classes are providing  of the sort of teacher support that  they're giving  obviously this is anecdotal but what has  your inspection  shown in terms of whether or not there  is consistency  about the amount of support schools have  provided over  over lockdown last summer when there  weren't  explicit requirements for schools it was  clear that there was enormous  variability  in the experience that children were  getting i think what our monitoring  visits this term have shown  um is that is is that we're getting much  closer  um to um consistency and in that there  are there are clear minimum expectations  of some quite significant input and that  the vast majority  of schools schools are putting that  effort in and the vast majority of  children  are receiving that having said which  there will always be corners  um whether whether problems i'm not  saying that every child in the country  has had the full experience they should  but but compared with last year  um it's a very very much better picture  but it is still tiring exhausting  draining for everybody  um i think if there's one thing we've  learned it's quite how important  schools actual schools physical schools  are for adults and for children  yeah i think that's certainly something  that parents and pupils wouldn't  disagree with you with and just finally  i know this isn't strictly your brief  but i just want to get your thoughts on  exams and do you think that having  teachers effectively deciding exam  results is the right way to go  there is no perfect solution this year  we have a system that's designed for  normal years when  all children have had more or less the  same access um to education  this year for obvious reasons it's been  very unequal  across across the country between  schools so there was a groundswell  of opinion that said that said it  wouldn't be fair simply to run exams as  normal  um because the results would recognize  that in would be as much about that  inequality and access as they would  about  um how well children have done and with  what they have been taught or what  potential they've got for progression  so we've got a hybrid here which is  i think i think is it is a good attempt  at creating  um the best we can do in in very very  unsatisfactory circumstances  and i think what what we need to do is  have everybody embrace it and do as much  as we can to make it work  okay amanda spielman thank you so much  for being on the program this morning  thank you  thank you

